"Rules of Practice and Procedure (https://www.cpuc.ca.gov/proceedings-and-rulemaking/rules-of-practice-and-procedure)\nciteturn1014search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; [2026-0804-01S - Approval_CPUC Rules Effective Jan 1 2027] These changes will be effective January 1, 2027.In the meantime, please continue to use the Rules of Practice and Procedure (effective 2021).\n\n# Rules of Practice and Procedure\n\n  * Rules of Practice and Procedure (effective May 2021)\n\nNotice of Proposed Changes to the Rules of Practice and Procedure\n\nIf you would like to be notified of proposed changes to the Rules of Practice and Procedure, you may sign up for the mailing list.\n\nThe Commission’s Rules of Practice and Procedure are modified from time to time, for example, to reflect new legislation and changes in best practices. If you would like to be notified when the Commission is proposing changes to its Rules of Practice and Procedure, please subscribe!\nSummary of Commission Orders Updating the Rules of Practice and Procedure (2001-Present)\n\n2026 Resolution ALJ-485 The Office of Administrative Law approved the changes adopted in Resolution ALJ-485, along with other nonsubstantive edits, on September 16, 2026. [2026-0804-01S - Approval_CPUC Rules Effective Jan 1 2027] These changes will be effective January 1, 2027. In the meantime, please continue to use the Rules of Practice and Procedure (effective 2021).\n\n2021 Resolution ALJ-398\n2017 Resolution ALJ-344\n2016 - D.16-01-017\n2015 - D.15-05-029\n2013 Resolution ALJ-290\n2011 Resolution ALJ-260\n2009 Resolution ALJ-224\n2006 - D.06-12-041\n2005 - D.05-11-015\n2005 - D.05-05-002\n2004 - D.04-07-013\nImage: General Process for Rules of Practice and Procedure Updates\n\n## Rules of Practice and Procedure\n\n  * California Public Utilities Commission Custom and Practice--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPractitioner's Page (https://www.cpuc.ca.gov/practitioners)\nciteturn1014search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; If you find information here that you believe to be out of date or think of a topic on which a FAQ or Custom and Practice sheet would be helpful, please contact practitioners@cpuc.ca.gov. ...   * Filing Fees | 2026‑27 Fiscal Year Filing Fees (effective Aug. ... For purposes of Rule 9.2(c), notice of the assignment is the day the assignment of the assigned ALJ appears in the Daily Calendar, consistent with prior practice.\n\n# California Public Utilities Commission Practitioner’s Page\n\nPractice before the Commission is governed by the Public Utilities Code, the Commission’s Rules of Practice and Procedure, specific Commission orders, and customs and practices developed over time to facilitate the efficient resolution of procedural matters that come up in the course of a proceeding.  This page is designed to put useful information for both new and veteran practitioners in one place for easy access. If you find information here that you believe to be out of date or think of a topic on which a FAQ or Custom and Practice sheet would be helpful, please contact practitioners@cpuc.ca.gov. We value your feedback.\n\n  * Alternative Dispute Resolution: Learn about our ADR program and how it can assist in creating better outcomes for your cases.\n  * Custom and Practice:  Guidelines and best practices to assist you in understanding the rules.     \n  * Electronic Filing\n  * Filing Fees | 2026‑27 Fiscal Year Filing Fees (effective Aug. 1, 2026)\n  * Mixed Media Filings: Cutting-Edge Filing Techniques to Accommodate Multiple & Voluminous Media Formats\n  * Ex Parte Communications  \n  * Filing Confidential Documents Under Seal (Motion to File Under Seal)\n  * Subscription Service\n  * Recently Published Documents: A search result of documents published by the CPUC, updated every 24 hours\n  * Rules of Practice and Procedure\n\nSubscribe to mailing list for Proposed Rule Changes\n\n## CPUC Practitioner Frequently Asked Questions\n\nStumped? Got a question? Try our FAQs!\n\n* * *\n\n  * Docket Office General FAQs: Docket Office handles the intake of all formal filings. If you have a question about the filing rules, try our Docket Office FAQs first.  \n  * Filing Public Versions of Redacted Documents  \n  * How to be Added to a Service List\n  * How to Change a Party Representative on the Service List: \n\nTo change the representative for a party on the service list, you can submit the change request through the from in the link below,\n\nhttps://www.cpuc.ca.gov/-/media/cpuc-website/divisions/administrative-law-judge-division/documents/additiontoservicelisttranscriptordercompliant.pdf\n\nthen, send it to the process office e-mail (processoffice@cpuc.ca.gov)\n\nOR, you can reach out directly to the process office e-mail with the request, without the form. The email should include all the same contact information required by the form.\n\n  * Subscription Service\n\n## Practitioner's Page\n\n  * Creating Formal Documents - Templates Provided\n  * E-File a Document\n  * Serving and Filing Proceeding Related Documents\n\nUPDATE 8/21/2026\nPlease be advised that the process of assigning Administrative Law Judges (ALJs) and Commissioners is changed to expedite the assignment process. ALJ and Commissioner assignments are now posted on the Daily Calendar and on the Proceeding’s Docket Card on a rolling basis. Preliminary categorization of proceedings still occurs through the ALJ 176 resolution process (item 1 of each Commission meeting agenda). \n  \nCommissioner and ALJ assignments can be found in a new section of the Daily Calendar called “Commissioner and Administrative Law Judge Assignments.” For purposes of Rule 9.2(c), notice of the assignment is the day the assignment of the assigned ALJ appears in the Daily Calendar, consistent with prior practice. \n  \nThis replaces the previous process of noticing Commissioner and Administrative Law Judge Assignments on the Daily Calendar after the preliminary categorization for the proceeding is approved by the Commission. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElectronic Waste Management - CalRecycle Home Page (https://calrecycle.ca.gov/electronics/)\nciteturn1014search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;   * On January 1, 2026, Senate Bill 1215 adds covered battery-embedded products to the Covered Electronic Waste Recycling Program. ... ### Approved Collectors and Recyclers ... For more information contact: Covered Electronic Waste Recycling Program, Ewaste@calrecycle.ca.gov\n\n# Electronic Waste Management\n\n \n\nEach year in California hundreds of thousands of computers, printers, televisions, and other electronics become outdated in the eyes of consumers. Rapid advances in technology and demand for new features and upgrades led to an increase of wasted electronic devices, or e-waste.\n\n#### Program News\n\n  * On January 1, 2026, Senate Bill 1215 adds covered battery-embedded products to the Covered Electronic Waste Recycling Program. For the implementation timeline and an overview of the bill requirements, visit the SB 1215 webpage.\n  * The law requires that, on or before July 1, 2025, and every year thereafter, a manufacturer of a covered battery-embedded product must send a notice with the product brand name and model number to all retailers in California selling those products (see Public Resources Code (PRC) section 42466.2). For more information, visit the Manufacturer Information webpage.\n  * CalRecycle compiled a list of covered battery-embedded products from notices sent to CalRecycle by manufacturers per PRC section 42466.2. \nCollector and Recycler Forms and Information\n\nDirectory of Approved Collectors and Recyclers of CEW\n\n### Local Governments\n\nRegulations governing the CEW Recycling Program contain a provision that allows a Local Government to designate an approved collector to provide its specified CEW collection services.\n\nGuidance and Resources\n\n### Manufacturers\n\nManufacturers of covered electronic devices are required annually to:\n\n  * Report specific information in Manufacturer Reports to CalRecycle about applicable devices that are sold in California\n  * Notify retailers in California of the fee requirement associated with selling these devices\n  * Provide information to consumers that describes where and how to return, recycle, or dispose of their devices\n\nLearn More\n\nThis page is for informational purposes only; it does not list all requirements in statute and regulations.\n\n#### Related Links\n\n  * Covered Electronic Waste Recycling Program\n  * Electronic Waste Recycling Fee\n  * Covered Battery-Embedded Products\n  * Manufacturer Notice Guidance\n  * Manufacturer Information\n\nFor more information contact: Covered Electronic Waste Recycling Program, Ewaste@calrecycle.ca.gov--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - 2026-02-06_Comments (https://docs.cpuc.ca.gov/PublishedDocs/Efile/G000/M598/K100/598100952.PDF)\nciteturn1014search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Pursuant to Rule 14.3 of the California Public Utilities Commission’s (Commission) Rules of Practice and Procedure, and the January 27, 2026 Email Ruling Granting, In Part, Request for Extension of Filing Deadlines for Proposed Decision (Rule 11.6), the California Independent System Operator Corporation (CAISO) hereby submits opening comments on the January 14, 2026 Proposed Decision Requiring 2029-2032 Electric Resource Procurement and Transmitting Portfolios for 2026-2027 Transmission Planning Process (Proposed Decision). ... The CAISO strongly supports the Proposed Decision’s recommendation to order procurement of 6 GW of new effective capacity.\nBEFORE THE PUBLIC UTILITIES COMMISSION  \nOF THE STATE OF CALIFORNIA\n\nOrder Instituting Rulemaking to  \nContinue Oversight of Electric  \nIntegrated Resource Planning and  \nProcurement Processes.  \n\nRulmaking 25-06-019\n\nOPENING COMMENTS OF THE  \nCALIFORNIA INDEPENDENT SYSTEM OPERATOR CORPORATION ON THE  \nPROPOSED DECISION REQUIRING 2029-2032 ELECTRIC RESOURCE  \nPROCUREMENT AND TRANSMITTING PORTFOLIOS FOR 2026-2027  \nTRANSMISSION PLANNING PROCESS\n\nI. Introduction\n\nPursuant to Rule 14.3 of the California Public Utilities Commission’s (Commission) Rules of Practice and Procedure, and the January 27, 2026 Email Ruling Granting, In Part, Request for Extension of Filing Deadlines for Proposed Decision (Rule 11.6), the California Independent System Operator Corporation (CAISO) hereby submits opening comments on the January 14, 2026 Proposed Decision Requiring 2029-2032 Electric Resource Procurement and Transmitting Portfolios for 2026-2027 Transmission Planning Process (Proposed Decision).\n\nThe CAISO supports several of the Proposed Decision’s directives. The CAISO appreciates the efforts of Energy Division staff to develop the proposed base and sensitivity portfolios and busbar mapping for study in the CAISO’s 2026-2027 Transmission Planning Process (TPP).\n\nThe CAISO supports the Proposed Decision’s recommended base case and Limited Wind Sensitivity portfolios for study in the CAISO’s 2026-2027 TPP. The CAISO strongly supports the Proposed Decision’s recommendation to order procurement of 6 GW of new effective capacity. Specifically, the CAISO supports the magnitude of the proposed procurement order to address reliability needs between 2029 and 2032. Issuing a procurement order now is both prudent and necessary to address reliability needs in the 2029-2032 timeframe. The CAISO, however, recommends the Commission adopt an alternative approach to phase in the 6 GW of new procurement to best ensure the ordered procurement covers system reliability needs\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFees Regulations | Board of Environmental Safety (https://bes.dtsc.ca.gov/fees-regulations/)\nciteturn1014search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; The California Board of Environmental Safety sets annual rates for DTSC’s Facility Fee, Generation and Handling Fee, and Environmental Fee through emergency rulemaking procedures no later than October 1 each year. ... Fee Rates FY 2026-27 – Final Approved Regulatory Text\n\n# Fee Rate Setting\n(Emergency Rulemaking)\n\nThe California Board of Environmental Safety sets annual rates for DTSC’s Facility Fee, Generation and Handling Fee, and Environmental Fee through emergency rulemaking procedures no later than October 1 each year.\n\n## FY 2026-27\n\nOn August 26, 2026, the Board set fee rates for the 202 6-2 7 fiscal year. \n\nResolution 2026-0001\nNotice of Proposed Emergency Action\nOAL File No. 2026-0915-02E – Notice of Approval\nFee Rates FY 2026-27 – Final Approved Regulatory Text\n\nTimeline of Rulemaking:\n\n  * Approved by the Board of Environmental Safety: August 26, 2026\n  * Submittal of emergency rulemaking to the Office of Administrative Law (OAL): September 15, 2026\n  * Approved by OAL: September 25, 2026\n  * Filed with the Secretary of State: September 25, 2026\n  * Effective date of the Regulations: October 1, 2026\n\n## FY 2025-26\n\nOn August 27, 2025, the Board set fee rates for the 2025-26 fiscal year.\n\nResolution 2025-0001\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCovered Battery-Embedded Product Q&A (https://www2.calrecycle.ca.gov/Docs/Web/137572)\nciteturn1014search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Crawled: 10 months ago; Approved Recyclers of CBEPs must weigh and issue separate weighmaster certificates for each treatment residual derived from cancellation of battery-embedded CEW. ... CalRecycle has no requirements for how batteries are shipped offsite. ... PRC section 42464(b)(1) requires that, on and after January 1, 2026, a consumer shall pay a covered battery-embedded waste recycling fee upon the purchase of a new or refurbished CBEP.\nQ. How long can batteries be stored at a recycling facility prior to shipment?\n\nA. Storage times for e-waste and potentially hazardous e-waste residuals fall under the purview of DTSC, and those questions should be directed to electronicwaste@dtsc.ca.gov.\n\nQ. Approved Recyclers of CBEPs must weigh and issue separate weighmaster certificates for each treatment residual derived from cancellation of battery-embedded CEW. Each battery chemistry is considered a separate residual. Does that mean that each battery chemistry must also be shipped offsite separately?\n\nA. CalRecycle has no requirements for how batteries are shipped offsite. The requirement is to weigh the total amount of batteries of each chemistry resulting from the claim, prior to shipment (see section 18660.26(e)(4) in the proposed regulations). The proposed regulations have not yet been approved by OAL. If approved as currently written, these proposed regulations are anticipated to go into effect on January 1, 2026. See the full text of the proposed regulations at the following link: SB 1215 Proposed Regulation Text.\n\nQ. What is the covered battery-embedded waste recycling fee?\n\nA. PRC section 42464(b)(1) requires that, on and after January 1, 2026, a consumer shall pay a covered battery-embedded waste recycling fee upon the purchase of a new or refurbished CBEP. CalRecycle’s proposed regulations provide, in part, that:\n\n“On or after January 1, 2026, 1.5 percent of the retail sales price for each covered battery-embedded product sold for use in this state by a retailer. The covered battery-embedded waste recycling fee shall not exceed $15.00. For the purposes of this section, “retail sales price” shall have the same meaning as “sales price” in section 6011 of the Revenue and Taxation Code”.\n\nFor example, 1.5% up to the cap of $15 means that any CBEP equal to or greater than $1,000 shall apply the covered battery-embedded waste recycling fee cap of $15 (See section 18660.40(d) in the proposed regulations). The proposed regulations have not yet been approved by OAL. If approved as currently written, these proposed regulations are anticipated to go into effect on January 1, 2026. See the full text of the proposed regulations at the following link: SB 1215 Proposed Regulation Text.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNotice of Covered Battery-Embedded Waste Recycling Fee Emergency Regulations Final OAL Approval (https://content.govdelivery.com/accounts/CALRECYCLE/bulletins/3ff7251)\nciteturn1014search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 5 days ago; On December 1, 2025, the Office of Administrative Law (OAL) approved the Covered Battery-Embedded Waste Recycling Fee emergency regulations which establish a covered battery-embedded waste recycling fee based on the reasonable regulatory costs to administer covered electronic waste recycling per Public Resources Code (PRC) section 42464(b)(3).The effective date of these regulations is January 1, 2026, and they will expire on January 1, 2028. ... Email (regulations@calrecycle.ca.gov)\n# Notice of Covered Battery-Embedded Waste Recycling Fee Emergency Regulations Final OAL Approval\n\nCalRecycle sent this bulletin at 12/11/2025 01:03 PM PST\n\n# Notice of Covered Battery-Embedded Waste Recycling Fee Emergency Regulations Final OAL Approval\n\nOn December 1, 2025, the Office of Administrative Law (OAL) approved the Covered Battery-Embedded Waste Recycling Fee emergency regulations which establish a covered battery-embedded waste recycling fee based on the reasonable regulatory costs to administer covered electronic waste recycling per Public Resources Code (PRC) section 42464(b)(3). The effective date of these regulations is January 1, 2026, and they will expire on January 1, 2028. Please visit the public notice and rulemaking webpage linked in the following section for more information about this rulemaking.\nPublic Notice\nRulemaking Webpage\n\n# Contact Us\n\nImage: Contact us by email\n\n### Emma Cervantes\n\n(916) 327-0089\nEmail (regulations@calrecycle.ca.gov)\nRulemaking Archives Webpage--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMicrosoft Word - A2304009-SCE Amended Application.docx (https://ia.cpuc.ca.gov/environment/info/panoramaenv/EPL_TLRR/Main/SCE_EPL_TLRR_A2304009-SCE_Amended_Application.pdf)\nciteturn1014search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; August 2024 September 2026 | August 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Scoping Memo | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- August 2024 September 2026 | September 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Concurrent Opening Testimony | | August 2024 September 2026 | September 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Concurrent Rebuttal Testimony | | August 2024 September 2026 | October 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Concurrent Opening Briefs Due | | August 2024 September 2026 | November 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Concurrent Reply Briefs Due | | August 2024 September 2026 January 20252027 | | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Proposed Decision Issued | | August 2024 September 2026 | February 2025 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Comments on Proposed Decision Due | | August 2024 September 2026 February 2025 | February 2025 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued | Reply Comments on Proposed Decision | August 2024 September 2026 None | | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued None | Due | August 2024 September 2026 March 20252027 | | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued CommissionFinal Decision | | ... Articles of Incorporation (CPUC Rule 2.2; Public Utilities Code § 1004)\nAugust 2024 September 2026 | August 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Scoping Memo | | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- August 2024 September 2026 | September 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Concurrent Opening Testimony | | August 2024 September 2026 | September 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Concurrent Rebuttal Testimony | | August 2024 September 2026 | October 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Concurrent Opening Briefs Due | | August 2024 September 2026 | November 2024 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Concurrent Reply Briefs Due | | August 2024 September 2026 January 20252027 | | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Proposed Decision Issued | | August 2024 September 2026 | February 2025 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued Comments on Proposed Decision Due | | August 2024 September 2026 February 2025 | February 2025 | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued | Reply Comments on Proposed Decision | August 2024 September 2026 None | | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued None | Due | August 2024 September 2026 March 20252027 | | | Prehearing ConferenceFinal CEQA Document Issued CommissionFinal Decision | | \nD. \nArticles of Incorporation (CPUC Rule 2.2; Public Utilities Code § 1004) \nPursuant to CPUC Rule 2.2 and Public Utilities Code Section 1004of the Commission’s \nRules of Practice and Procedure, a copy of SCE’s Certificate of Amended and Restated Articles \nof Incorporation, as effective on March 2August 28, 20062023, and as presently in effect, \ncertified by the California Secretary of State, was filed with the Commission on March \n14December 15, 20062023, in connection with Application No. 06-03-020, and is byA.23-12-\n011, and is incorporated herein by this reference made a part hereof. A statement of SCE’s \ncorporate information is attached hereto as AppendixAPPENDIX E. \nE. Deposit for Costs (CPUC Rule 2.5) \nPursuant to Rule 1.16 of the Commission’s Rules of Practice and Procedure, SCE will \nsubmit a filing fee of $500.00 to the CPUC Docket Office along with this Application. CPUC \nRule 2.5 provides that an applicant include a deposit, to be applied to the costs the Commission \nincurs to prepare a negative declaration or an environmental impact report, when the \n \n20 \n \nAppendix H - Page 27--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBattery-Embedded Products Informal Rulemaking Workshop (https://www2.calrecycle.ca.gov/PublicNotices/Documents/15944)\nciteturn1014search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.1 years ago; Crawled: 2.1 years ago; (e) Prior to April 1, 2026, Aan approved recycler shall deliver recycling payment claims to CalRecycle's main business office, to the attention of the Accounting Section.\nGeneral Requirements for Recycling Payment Claims\n\n14 CCR Section 18660.22\n\n...\n\n(a)(7) The sSignature and title of an person with signature authority Authorized Signatory for payment claims as designated pursuant to Section 18660.11 of this Chapter...\n\n...\n\n(e) Prior to April 1, 2026, Aan approved recycler shall deliver recycling payment claims to CalRecycle's main business office, to the attention of the Accounting Section. An approved recycler shall mark the outside of the package containing the claims clearly with \"Covered Electronic Waste Claim Enclosed.\" On and after April 1, 2026, all recycling payment claims shall be submitted using CalRecycle’s electronic portal system pursuant to Section 18660.7.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBattery Stewardship - CalRecycle Home Page (https://calrecycle.ca.gov/epr/batteries/)\nciteturn1014search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; CalRecycle held an informal rulemaking workshop on June 16, 2026, to solicit feedback on the proposed regulations to implement the Responsible Battery Recycling Act. ... Program operators must have an approved stewardship plan. ...   * Contact EmbeddedBatteries@calrecycle.ca.gov\n\n# Battery Stewardship\n\n \n\nThe Responsible Battery Recycling Act holds the battery industry responsible for the collection, recycling, and proper end-of-life management of covered batteries.\n\n#### Program News\n\nCalRecycle held an informal rulemaking workshop on June 16, 2026, to solicit feedback on the proposed regulations to implement the Responsible Battery Recycling Act. Visit the public notice for more information.\n\nGet Email Updates About Battery Stewardship\n\nSee all CalRecycle lists and previous messages.\n\nBatteries can harm human health and the environment when not managed properly.\n\n  * An estimated 7,294 tons of batteries are improperly disposed of in California landfills annually.\n  * Batteries are the top cause of fires in California’s waste facilities, endangering lives and costing millions of dollars in damage.\n  * Batteries contain valuable and rare metals like copper, nickel, and lithium. \n#### 120 Days After Regulations are Effective\n\nCalRecycle will appoint an advisory body.\n\nImage\n\n#### 12 Months After Regulations are Effective\n\nProgram operators submit a stewardship plan to CalRecycle.\n\nImage\n\n#### 24 Months After Regulations are Effective\n\nProgram operators must have an approved stewardship plan.\n\nImage\n\n#### 12 Months After Plan Approval\n\nProgram operators must fully implement the approved stewardship plan.\n\n#### Resources\n\n  * Earth 911: Where To Drop Off Batteries\n  * List of Covered Battery Producers and Brands (updated June 2023)\n  * DTSC: Universal Waste Batteries\n  * DTSC: Recycling Rechargeable Batteries\n  * Waste Banned from the Trash\n  * Your Local Curbside Recycling Information\n  * US EPA Memorandum: Lithium Battery Recycling Regulatory Status and FAQ\n\n#### Related Links\n\n  * Extended Producer Responsibility (EPR) \n  * Responsible Battery Recycling Act Regulations\n\n \n\n#### Embedded Batteries\n\n  * SB 1215 Covered Battery-Embedded Products\n  * Contact EmbeddedBatteries@calrecycle.ca.gov \n  * Where Do I Recycle E-Waste?\n\nFor more information, contact Batteries@calrecycle.ca.gov.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVideo Franchise Workshops (https://www.cpuc.ca.gov/regulatory-services/licensing/video-franchising/video-franchise-workshops)\nciteturn1014search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; T he CPUC can also consider potential ways to modernize and make the implementation of DIVCA more efficient and effective.Rule 7.5 (a)(2) of the CPUC’s Rules of Practice and Procedure requires the C PUC host “[a]t least one workshop providing an opportunity for the parties to the proceeding to have an interactive discussion on issues identified in the scoping memo.” ...   * August 17, 2026 starting at 10 a.m.\n\n# Video Franchise Workshops\n\n## Video Franchise Workshops\n\nThe Digital Infrastructure and Video Competition Act of 2006 (DIVCA) streamlined the video franchising process by placing the state’s franchising authority under just one government agency, the California Public Utilities Commission (CPUC). The CPUC is the sole entity responsible for determining which companies are qualif ied to operate as state video franchise holders in California.\nOn April 17, 2023, the CPUC opened Rulemaking (R.) 23-04-006 to consider changes to General Order 169: Implementing DIVCA. The CPUC must also adopt consumer protection rules, adjudicate customer complaints, and more detailed reporting requirements regarding video services. T he CPUC can also consider potential ways to modernize and make the implementation of DIVCA more efficient and effective.\nRule 7.5 (a)(2) of the CPUC’s Rules of Practice and Procedure requires the C PUC host “[a]t least one workshop providing an opportunity for the parties to the proceeding to have an interactive discussion on issues identified in the scoping memo.” --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCompliance With Ordering Paragraphs (https://ia.cpuc.ca.gov/cops/customsearch2.aspx)\nciteturn1014search7 [wordlim: